WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.915
Welcome to Catalyst. This walkthrough takes you from
getting the software to

00:00:04.915 --> 00:00:09.480
a useful learning session. We will follow one idea:
how changing a

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:14.045
function changes its graph, and why algebraic steps
work. Everything shown uses

00:00:14.045 --> 00:00:15.567
a separate demo learner.

00:00:16.717 --> 00:00:22.099
Start with the Catalyst offline ZIP supplied with this
project. It includes

00:00:22.099 --> 00:00:27.482
the app and all the rendered lesson videos. The
smaller source ZIP

00:00:27.482 --> 00:00:32.864
contains the code, transcripts and exercises. Extract
the archive into a normal

00:00:32.864 --> 00:00:38.247
folder before opening anything. Catalyst's original
code and shared lessons use the

00:00:38.247 --> 00:00:43.630
included MIT license. The upstream repository is
currently private, so use the

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:47.667
provided archive rather than expecting a public GitHub
download.

00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:52.939
Install Python three point ten or newer from python
dot org. On

00:00:52.939 --> 00:00:57.061
Windows, open the extracted Catalyst folder and double
click Start Catalyst. If

00:00:57.061 --> 00:01:01.183
Python is not found, finish Python setup and check
that the python

00:01:01.183 --> 00:01:05.305
command works. On Mac or Linux, open a terminal in
that folder

00:01:05.305 --> 00:01:09.427
and run python three, space, start dot py. Keep the
terminal open.

00:01:09.427 --> 00:01:13.549
In your browser, open the local address shown here.
Stop the server

00:01:13.549 --> 00:01:15.267
with Control C when finished.

00:01:16.417 --> 00:01:20.364
Choose the learner name to create your own learning
space. Pick a

00:01:20.364 --> 00:01:24.311
starting path and how you like to work. These
preferences choose your

00:01:24.311 --> 00:01:28.257
first activity; they do not give you a test score.
Each learner

00:01:28.257 --> 00:01:32.204
has separate saved work on this computer. You can
return without starting

00:01:32.204 --> 00:01:32.533
over.

00:01:33.683 --> 00:01:38.110
If you are unsure where to begin, choose Find my
level. The

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:42.537
assessment asks one question at a time and adapts
across several areas.

00:01:42.537 --> 00:01:46.964
Say when you do not know. Its recommendation is a
starting estimate,

00:01:46.964 --> 00:01:51.391
not a permanent label. You can also browse Courses
directly, from number

00:01:51.391 --> 00:01:52.867
sense through college topics.

00:01:54.017 --> 00:01:58.407
Open a topic and watch its short narrated animation.
Pause, replay, or

00:01:58.407 --> 00:02:02.797
use captions and chapters. Then open the study guide
when one is

00:02:02.797 --> 00:02:07.187
available. Read the worked example and ask what
changed at each line,

00:02:07.187 --> 00:02:11.577
and why. Here, a function is a rule: changing the
input changes

00:02:11.577 --> 00:02:15.967
the output. Prerequisite links help repair a missing
step before you continue.

00:02:17.117 --> 00:02:22.013
The playground now brings the interactive tools
together. Free exploration lets you

00:02:22.013 --> 00:02:26.910
change a graph or diagram. Guided reasoning explains
valid transformations. Applied models

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:31.807
connect several ideas to a situation. In the function
lab, predict what

00:02:31.807 --> 00:02:36.703
changing the coefficient will do. Adjust it, watch the
graph move, and

00:02:36.703 --> 00:02:41.600
compare the result with your prediction. Change one
thing at a time.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:47.488
When a simplification feels like a jump, open Why this
step. Reveal

00:02:47.488 --> 00:02:52.225
one transformation and its reason at a time. In the
expression comparison,

00:02:52.225 --> 00:02:56.963
the quotient simplifies to a line everywhere except
the excluded input. The

00:02:56.963 --> 00:03:01.701
missing point still matters. Matching graph shapes is
a useful observation; factoring

00:03:01.701 --> 00:03:06.439
and the domain restriction explain why the expressions
agree where both are

00:03:06.439 --> 00:03:06.833
defined.

00:03:07.983 --> 00:03:12.096
Return to Practice and try the three questions. Start
with Build the

00:03:12.096 --> 00:03:16.209
idea, then Connect the steps and Stretch and transfer.
Write your method

00:03:16.209 --> 00:03:20.322
as well as your answer. If you are stuck, ask for a

00:03:20.322 --> 00:03:24.434
hint. Check the feedback, find the first uncertain
step, and try another

00:03:24.434 --> 00:03:26.833
variation. Getting help is part of learning.

00:03:27.983 --> 00:03:32.764
When you can explain the method, take a fresh
independent check. Hinted

00:03:32.764 --> 00:03:37.545
or previously exposed questions do not establish new
independent readiness. A passed

00:03:37.545 --> 00:03:42.326
check is useful evidence, but one short session does
not prove lasting

00:03:42.326 --> 00:03:47.106
mastery. Some written work needs a tutor's review.
Watching a video alone

00:03:47.106 --> 00:03:48.700
never passes a lesson.

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:54.506
Apply the math connects ideas in a real model. Choose
an investigation,

00:03:54.506 --> 00:03:59.161
make a prediction, change a control, and explain what
you observe. The

00:03:59.161 --> 00:04:03.817
motion model links position, time and rate of change.
Worked examples prepare

00:04:03.817 --> 00:04:08.472
you for three practice questions and a larger written
project. State your

00:04:08.472 --> 00:04:10.800
assumptions and units as you reason.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:17.179
My homework brings your saved writing together. Review
my reasoning helps you

00:04:17.179 --> 00:04:22.407
inspect assumptions, transformations, notation and
conclusions. Save a revised explanation and say

00:04:22.407 --> 00:04:27.636
what changed. Earlier versions stay available. You can
copy your work for

00:04:27.636 --> 00:04:32.864
a tutor, or open the optional tailoring workspace with
your actual saved

00:04:32.864 --> 00:04:33.300
explanation.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:39.113
Mixed review gives fresh three-question sets across
supported skills and suggests later

00:04:39.113 --> 00:04:43.776
retrieval. An empty due list is normal before you have
qualifying work

00:04:43.776 --> 00:04:48.439
to review. Tailor to my interests connects a topic
with your goals.

00:04:48.439 --> 00:04:53.103
Its copyable tutor brief works without AI. An optional
local Ollama model

00:04:53.103 --> 00:04:56.600
can offer unverified help; it does not grade you.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:02.726
Keep this rhythm: understand, predict, explore, try,
explain, and return later. Move

00:05:02.726 --> 00:05:07.701
quickly when the evidence supports it, and revisit
foundations whenever a step

00:05:07.701 --> 00:05:12.677
feels unclear. Your progress stays on disk. Use the
written getting-started guide

00:05:12.677 --> 00:05:17.652
for setup and backup instructions. Begin with one
idea, and make it

00:05:17.652 --> 00:05:18.067
yours.
